t  thank you  our next question um looks like this one  is for grant it asks is it possible to  test that the simulation is working with  flash and freeze and zap and freeze  uh yeah so for  uh flash this is  relatively trivial in the senses it's  the same kind of controls you do for any  other channel red ops experiment you  know you just have whatever your  configuration is whatever your  preparation is expressing  channelrhodopsin uh you know do the same  stimulation you do for flash and freeze  but do your e-fits recordings the same  kind of thing you'd normally do for uh  to measure action potentials for general  reduction for zap and freeze it's a  little trickier because  unlike unlike flash and freeze where  you're just doing channel adoption  simulation the the field simulation is a  specific configuration  um so the the zap itself as it works in  the instrument only happens in this  closed sandwich in this closed thing  that's not accessible to ifas obviously  because it's a closed system you can't  put any electrodes in there um  and not really accessible optically to  things like measuring directly measuring  action potentials um that being said  there are two ways around that that  we've gotten around that so you can do  some other meth so we've stimulated  cells for example directly in the  uh with the system and then  um done some less precise experiments  just to check that you get activity so  for example you can do fm dye so we did  that we stimulated them in the machine  then instead of freezing them we do fm  dye  uh we uh uh fix them and then do fm die  imaging after that uh i think other  people have done stuff like calcium  imaging to check that it's working um  but if you want to really directly  measure your action pencils and say you  know i'm getting the exact pattern of  action potential that i want to uh you  can just use a uh field simulation  configuration of the same type you know  with the same field strength that kind  that you'd use for uh live imaging or  ephes recordings like that as a point of  comparison  all right thank you grant we have  another question here looks like this  one is directed towards you as well  this one asks when do you use the flash  and freeze method versus the zapping  freeze  uh yeah so so i focused on in my case  where zap and freeze was uh enabling of  the technology over flash and freeze  because of the um added temporal  precision uh so obviously that's not  going to apply to all experiments uh you  know much of the time you won't be  freezing at these kind of very so if you  want to look at transient docking you  got to do zap that's as far as we know  what what we know right now but for  other things especially if you're  freezing at longer time points  everything looks little for example  we've investigated ultra fast under  cytosis with zap and it looks just like  it does with flash  um  if you're using the same system i was  showing culture hippocampal neurons that  just seems like the easier thing you  don't have to worry about generatops and  you don't have to do any of that it's  just a simpler system  uh where flash can be really important  is obviously it gives you all the kind  of advantages that you get uh with  channelrhodopsin in neuroscience  generally that is if you're working in  an intact organism or you're working in  slices and you want to stimulate  specific pathways and know what you're  trying you know stimulate specific  neurons then you're obviously going to  use flash so it's going to be good for a  lot of systems like that um because  apart from the sort of things i  discussed the differences are mostly uh  subtle it's just going to be what's the  best for your system in terms of what  you're trying to investigate  great thank you looks like we have time  for one more question here and this is a  two-part question so this asks can this  high-pressure method be used for every  type of cell and can it get these  beautiful images from every type of cell  or is their cell favorite types  okay so  yeah and technically speaking you can  use this technique to look at any types  of cells  the only issue is that there's actually  a limit to  the thickness of the tissue that you can  breathe  and so typically this is supposed to be  about 200 micrometer and that basically  minimizes the  ice crystal formation and then you get  you know pretty decent images um  decent preservation of tissues  out of those samples  um  yeah so yeah you can use any type of any  types of cells  um and can you get beautiful images from  every type of cell so this is actually  an interesting question because it  really depends on the users  and so you have to really practice on  loading the samples handling the samples  in order to get a beautiful images and  so it takes quite quite a bit of  practice  but once you get used to doing a  breathing uh i think it's gonna be you  know  yeah this technique is to work and we  can always get good images  great well thank you again shigeki and  grant for your time today and your  important research we would also like to  thank labrutz and our sponsor like leica  microsystems for underwriting today's  educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we do not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  routes will alert you via email when  it's available for replay we encourage  you to share that email with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event  until next time take care everyone  goodbye  you

